&lt;h2 id=&#34;mechanics&#34;&gt;Mechanics&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&#34;newtons-laws&#34;&gt;Newton&amp;rsquo;s laws&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;(Inertia): an object will remain at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless acted upon by an external force.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;($F=ma$) acceleration is caused by force.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;(Reaction) for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&#34;energies&#34;&gt;Energies&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Work is defined as the dot product of force and displacement: $W = \vec{F}\cdot\vec{s}$. For a constant force acting in the direction of a straight line, $W=Fs$. For varying forces $F=F(x)$ on a straight line, work is the integral of $F$ w.r.t. $x$:
$$
W = \int_{x_1}^{x_2}F(x)dx
$$
The SI unit of work is joule (J), $1 J = 1 N\cdot m$. From
$$
a=\frac{dv}{dt}=\frac{dv}{dx}\frac{dx}{dt}=v\cdot\frac{dv}{dx},
$$
the kinetic energy is defined as
$$
K = \int Fdx = \int madx = m\int v\frac{dv}{dx}dx = m\int vdv = \frac{1}{2}mv^2
$$
and the work-energy theorem is
$$
W_{\text{tot}} = K_2 - K_1 = \Delta K.
$$&lt;/p&gt;</description>
